      "problem": "Express $\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.",
      "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+7)}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+7$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+17=(Ax+B)(x+1)+C({x}^{2}+7).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}+2(-1)+17 & =(A(-1)+B)(-1+1)+C((-1)^{2}+7) \\\\ 1-2+17 & =8C \\\\ C & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+2(0)+17 & =(A(0)+B)(0+1)+C({0}^{2}+7) \\\\ 17 & =B+7C \\\\ 17 & =B+7(2) \\\\ B & =17-14 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}+2(1)+17 & =(A(1)+B)(1+1)+C({1}^{2}+7) \\\\ 1+2+17 & =2A+2B+8C \\\\ 20 & =2A+2(3)+8(2) \\\\ 2A & =20-22 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1,$ $B=3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}=\\frac{-x+3}{({x}^{2}+7)}+\\frac{2}{(x+1)}.\n$$",
