      "name": null,
      "problem": "Express $\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+13}{({x}^{2}+5)(2x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.",
      "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+13}{({x}^{2}+5)(2x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+5)}+\\frac{C}{(2x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+5$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+5)(2x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+4x+13=(Ax+B)(2x-1)+C({x}^{2}+5).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+4(\\frac{1}{2})+13 & =(A(\\frac{1}{2})+B)(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+C({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+5) \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+2+13 & =C(\\frac{21}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{3+60}{4} & =C(\\frac{21}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{63}{4} & =C(\\frac{21}{4}) \\\\ C & =\\frac{63}{21} \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}+4(0)+13 & =(A(0)+B)(2(0)-1)+C({0}^{2}+5) \\\\ 13 & =-B+5C \\\\ 13 & =-B+5(3) \\\\ B & =15-13 \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}+4(1)+13 & =(A(1)+B)(2(1)-1)+C({1}^{2}+5) \\\\ 20 & =A+B+6C \\\\ 20 & =A+(2)+6(3) \\\\ A & =20-2-18 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=0,$ $B=2,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+13}{({x}^{2}+5)(2x-1)}=\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+5)}+\\frac{3}{(2x-1)}.\n$$",
